
In section 5, the term "Annual Income" refers to an individual's
single-year after-tax income (corrected for depreciation and sale of joint
assets) based upon the prior year's tax return.

5.3

In the current year Wife is to receive a minimum of 40% of the sum of both
parties' Annual Income.

If Wife's Annual Income exceeds this amount, no spousal support is payable
in the current year.

If Wife's Annual Income is less than this amount, Husband is ordered to
make up the difference during the current year.  The lesser of $100,000
per annum or 40% net income will be paid in monthly installments,
terminating automatically on the death of either party, Wife's remarriage,
or Wife's registration as a domestic partner or its equivalent in any
state or county, or until modified or terminated by further order or
written agreement, whichever event occurs first.  Any arrears, or
adjustments due to Husband, will be paid in a manner agreed to by Husband
and Wife.

5.4

Wife is under an obligation to notify Husband in the event she remarries
or registers as a domestic partner or its equivalent in any state or
county.  If Wife continues to accept spousal support payments after either
of those terminating events occurs, she shall refund all of those payments
to Husband with interest at 10% per annum until repaid in full.

5.5

Spousal Support Cap.  Notwithstanding anything to the contrary in this
Judgment, in no event shall Husband ever be required to pay Wife more than
40% of Annual Income in spousal support in any calendar year, which the
parties agree is the cap on spousal support according to Wifes marital
standard of living.

5.6

Spousal Support Arrears.  Husband owes Wife spousal support arrears based
upon 40% of net income (corrected for depreciation and sale of joint
assets) from the date of separation to present.  This amount will reflect
a credit to Husband for direct payments he made for Wifes expenses,
payments he advanced for joint expenses, and credits to Wife for payments
she made for the parties joint expenses.  Arrears or adjustment payment
will be made as tax returns are filed in a manner agreed to by Husband and
Wife: This may involve cash, shared mortgage paydown, or adjustment of
real estate ownership percentage.
